crossover-wheel, the in-bottle proxy.

t150-dinput8.dll is a DirectInput 8 proxy for a CrossOver bottle. It hands
everything to CrossOver's builtin dinput8 and wraps only the T150's force
feedback, which it forwards to the t150d daemon on the macOS side. Other
devices pass through untouched, and with no daemon answering it wraps
nothing and is transparent.

It loads in a real CrossOver bottle and chain-loads the builtin behind it;
that was proven with regsvr32 under loader tracing. What has never run is
the force feedback path: no game has created a device through it and no
effect has crossed to the daemon yet.

It serves 64-bit games only: a 32-bit process is redirected to syswow64 and
never sees it.

Install it as <bottle>/drive_c/windows/system32/dinput8.dll, with CrossOver's
builtin copied alongside as dinput8_orig.dll, and set dinput8 to
"native,builtin". Copy the builtin from CrossOver's own
lib/wine/x86_64-windows, never the dinput8.dll already in the bottle's
system32, which is an empty placeholder. Set T150_DEBUG=1 in the bottle to
make it say what it is doing on stderr, and T150_LOG to a file path to have
every process append the same lines there, which is the only way to see it
from a game Steam relaunched.

Input is forwarded untouched. The T150 labels its pedals against the
common convention, brake on Y and accelerator on Rz, and rests a released
pedal at maximum, but a game that binds pedals by asking you to press them
resolves both by itself, so the proxy leaves them alone by default. If a
game assumes the convention and cannot rebind, set T150_PEDALS to swap for
the labels, invert for the rest position, or full for both. The proxy logs
its version and these settings when it wraps the wheel.

With T150_LOG set it also records what a game asks of it: GetCapabilities,
the EnumEffects filter, every CreateEffect with its axis count and parameter
size, and the first Start of each effect. That log is what tells you whether
a game is asking for force feedback at all, which is the one thing nobody
has been able to see.

probe_dinput.exe is in this archive too, and it is the tool to reach for
when anything about the wheel looks wrong inside the bottle. Run it in the
bottle with no arguments and it prints what DirectInput says the device is:
every axis and button with its offset, the axle number a game's
configuration records, the declared range, and whether any axis is marked
as a force feedback actuator.

  wine --bottle "<name>" --cx-app probe_dinput.exe

Every run writes itself to Z:\tmp\probe_dinput.log, which is
/tmp/probe_dinput.log seen from macOS, as well as to the screen. Send that
file rather than a copied terminal: it is complete, it survives the window
being closed, and -o puts it somewhere else if you prefer.

Add -i and it works through every control by name, one at a time: the
wheel, each pedal, both paddles, all thirteen buttons and the four hat
directions. For each it says which DirectInput object moved, and for a
pedal it records where it rests and how far it travels, so an inverted
pedal is three numbers rather than an impression. It asks you to confirm
each identification, Enter for yes, n if it named the wrong thing, s to
skip a control this wheel does not have, and it ends with a table of the
whole wheel. Anything you disputed is flagged in that table, and that is
the line worth sending. Add -f and it creates a real
constant force and a real damper and plays them, with no game involved, so
a game that asks wrongly can be told apart from a chain that does not
deliver. Hold the wheel when you use -f.

Run it again with --dll dinput8=b added to the wine command to measure
CrossOver's builtin instead of this proxy; the difference between the two
runs is exactly what the proxy does.

The README in the repository has the exact commands as CrossOver 26
resolves them, and docs/PROBES.md question 8 is the test.
